Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below are the columns that matter (with a short note of why we might use them):

• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data.value` : the actual numeric value of every economic / social indicator (GDP, in our case).  
• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data.indicator_code` : lets us keep only the GDP‑constant‑2015 series (`NY.GDP.MKTP.KD`).  
• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data.year` : optional (to filter to the latest year or a particular period).  
• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data.country_code` : foreign key to country metadata.  
• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary.country_code` : join key.  
• `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary.region` : the World‑Bank region we need to rank by median GDP.

Typical filters / join conditions we will need  
```
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` AS d
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary` AS c
  ON d.country_code = c.country_code
WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'         -- GDP (constant 2015 US$)
  AND c.region IS NOT NULL                        -- keep only “real” regions
```


Ten exploratory → increasingly sophisticated queries
-----------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1) List all indicator codes containing the word GDP (quick scan)
SELECT DISTINCT indicator_code
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data`
WHERE LOWER(indicator_code) LIKE '%gdp%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Display the official name of NY.GDP.MKTP.KD so we’re sure it is GDP (constant‑2015)
SELECT DISTINCT indicator_name
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data`
WHERE indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Look at a few USA rows for quick sanity‑check of magnitudes
SELECT year, value
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data`
WHERE country_code = 'USA' AND indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
ORDER BY year DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) What regions exist in country metadata?
SELECT DISTINCT region
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary`
WHERE region IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY region
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) How many GDP (constant‑2015) rows per region (all country–year records)?
SELECT c.region, COUNT(*) AS rows_per_region
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` AS d
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary`  AS c
  ON d.country_code = c.country_code
WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
  AND c.region IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY c.region
ORDER BY rows_per_region DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Compute an APPROX median GDP per region (all years, all countries)
SELECT
  c.region,
  APPROX_QUANTILES(d.value, 2)[OFFSET(1)] AS approx_median_gdp
FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` AS d
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary`  AS c
  ON d.country_code = c.country_code
WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
  AND c.region IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY c.region
ORDER BY approx_median_gdp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Same, but keep only the most recent year available for every country
WITH latest AS (
  SELECT country_code, MAX(year) AS max_year
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data`
  WHERE indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
  GROUP BY country_code
)
SELECT
  c.region,
  APPROX_QUANTILES(d.value, 2)[OFFSET(1)] AS approx_median_latest_gdp
FROM latest
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` d
  ON latest.country_code = d.country_code
 AND latest.max_year    = d.year
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary` c
  ON d.country_code = c.country_code
WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
  AND c.region IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY c.region
ORDER BY approx_median_latest_gdp DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Exact median using PERCENTILE_CONT (all values) – BigQuery supports this analytic fn
SELECT
  region,
  PERCENTILE_CONT(value, 0.5) OVER (PARTITION BY region) AS exact_median_gdp,
  value
FROM (
  SELECT
    c.region,
    d.value
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` d
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary` c
    ON d.country_code = c.country_code
  WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
    AND c.region IS NOT NULL
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Collapse previous query to one row per region (exact median)
SELECT
  region,
  PERCENTILE_CONT(value, 0.5) OVER (PARTITION BY region) AS exact_median_gdp
FROM (
  SELECT
    c.region,
    d.value
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data` d
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary` c
    ON d.country_code = c.country_code
  WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
    AND c.region IS NOT NULL
)
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY region) = 1
ORDER BY exact_median_gdp DESC;
```

```sql
-- 10) Final concise answer: region with the highest median GDP (constant 2015 US$)
SELECT region,
       PERCENTILE_CONT(value, 0.5) OVER (PARTITION BY region) AS exact_median_gdp
FROM (
  SELECT c.region, d.value
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.indicators_data`  AS d
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.world_bank_wdi.country_summary`  AS c
        ON d.country_code = c.country_code
  WHERE d.indicator_code = 'NY.GDP.MKTP.KD'
    AND c.region IS NOT NULL
)
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY exact_median_gdp DESC) = 1;
```

Run the last query to see the single region that tops the ranking by median GDP (constant 2015 US$).
